    "findings": [
      "All 515 timed rows satisfy success, complete output, finite errors, 17 sampled errors and the common 1e-5 absolute-error threshold. This is sampled numerical eligibility, not rigorous continuous global accuracy.",
      "Among the original seven primary models, only Riccati has priced/fixed improvement (fixed/priced median 1.082). The other six remain slower than unconditional forward. The separately registered basis1 projection-failure stress case gives 1.115 versus fixed, while legacy is faster than priced there (legacy/priced 0.852).",
      "Priced cadence improves retained legacy medians on broad (1.054) and changing-rate (1.271) but is not faster than the fastest applicable protected solver on any primary or stress case. This is partial recovery of prior regression, not general production speed qualification.",
      "Two-worker Picard/residual-first shows synthetic expensive-RHS gains over the same algorithm at one worker (about 1.237-1.436), while cheap few-mode and Riccati are slower with two workers. Pool construction is amortized/excluded. Do not generalize synthetic gains to arbitrary applications.",
      "The final algorithm revision was evaluated rather than replaced after these mixed results; no fourth tuning loop is justified or performed by this review."
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "Five repetitions in one process, no independent-process confidence interval",
      "Analytic f64 references and sampled absolute-error eligibility",
      "Structured protected controls receive explicit analytic Jacobians; callback capabilities differ visibly",
      "Standalone root extension excludes banded arm and must be read with primary strongest-control results",
      "No new external VODE, diffsol or Julia comparison"
    ]
  },
